he test for \"handles multiple items with discount and tax\" only asserts `expect(result.total).toBe(130.86)`. It doesn't check discountAmount or subtotal or taxAmount. Only total.\n\nAnd the test for \"applies percentage discount before tax\" asserts `expect(result.total).toBe(97.2)`.\n\nAnd \"penny-edge amounts\" asserts `expect(result.total).toBe(33.88)`.\n\nSo only total is checked in these tests. But the PriceBreakdown struct does have components that should probably be reasonable.\n\nOK, so the fix is: don't round `afterDiscount` or `taxAmount`. Round only the final `total`.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the code:\n\n```js\nconst discountAmount = Math.round(subtotal * (discountPercent / 100) * 100) / 100;\nconst afterDiscount = Math.round((subtotal - discountAmount) * 100) / 100;\nconst taxAmount = Math.round(afterDiscount * taxRate * 100) / 100;\nconst total = afterDiscount + taxAmount;\n```\n\nSo the fix should be:\n```js\nconst discountAmount = Math.round(subtotal * (discountPercent / 100) * 100) / 100;\nconst afterDiscount = (subtotal - discountAmount); // DON'T ROUND\nconst taxAmount = afterDiscount * taxRate; // DON'T ROUND\nconst total = Math.round((afterDiscount + taxAmount) * 100) / 100; // ROUND FINAL TOTAL\n```\n\nWait, but `afterDiscount` uses `discountAmount` (rounded). So `afterDiscount = 139.96 - 20.99 = 118.97`. Then `taxAmount = 118.97 * 0.1 = 11.897`. Then `total = 118.97 + 11.897 = 130.867`. `Math.round(13086.7) / 100 = 13087 / 100 = 130.87`. That's still wrong.\n\nHmm, I think the actual intended calculation is:\n- DON'T round discountAmount either for the intermediate calculation\n- Only round the final display values\n\nLet me think about this differently. What if the intended computation is:\n- discountAmount = subtotal * (discountPercent / 100) (no intermediate rounding)\n- afterDiscount = subtotal - discountAmount (no intermediate rounding)\n- taxAmount = afterDiscount * taxRate (no intermediate rounding)\n- total = afterDiscount +