OrderService flow, `eventWithItems.status` is undefined, and the handler returns early without releasing stock.\n\nThe fix should either:\n1. Add `status: 'cancelled'` to the cancelOrder emit in OrderService, OR\n2. Remove the `status !== 'cancelled'` check from InventoryService\n\nSince we're told not to modify tests, and tests 3 and 4 emit events directly with `status: 'cancelled'`, the tests are correct. But the OrderService doesn't emit that field. The cleanest fix is to add `status: 'cancelled'` to the OrderService.cancelOrder emit.\n\nWait, but looking at the inventory test \"releases stock on order.cancelled\", it emits the event directly through the bus with `status: 'cancelled'`. The test expects it to work. That test does pass in isolation (since the bus emission has status). But when called through OrderService, the emitted event lacks status, so stock doesn't get released.\n\nAnd the test \"releases stock when order is cancelled\" in orders.test.ts calls:\n```ts\nawait orderService.cancelOrder('O1', 'changed mind');\n```\nwhich internally emits CANCELLED without status. This causes the inventory handler to return early.\n\nSo the fix is to add `status: 'cancelled'` to the cancelOrder emit in OrderService.\n\nActually, wait — there's another issue. In orders.test.ts, test \"releases stock when order is cancelled\":\n```ts\nit('releases stock when order is cancelled', async () => {\n    inventoryService.setStock('SKU-1', 10);\n    await orderService.createOrder('O1', 'C1', [\n      { productId: 'SKU-1', quantity: 4, unitPrice: 10 },\n    ]);\n    await orderService.cancelOrder('O1', 'changed mind');\n    const stock = inventoryService.getStock('SKU-1');\n    expect(stock?.available).toBe(10);\n    expect(stock?.reserved).toBe(0);\n});\n```\n\nThe flow: createOrder → emits CREATED → inventory reserves 4 → stock: available=6, reserved=4. Then cancelOrder → emits CANCELLED without status → inventory handler returns early due to status check → stock stays at available=6, reserved=4.\n\nThe fix: add `status: 'cancelled'` to the cancellation event emitted in OrderService.cancelOrder.\n\n## Bug 6: OrderService.cancelOrder referencing undefined items\n\n**File:** `src/orders/service.ts`, line 78\n**Code:** `items: order.items.map((i) => ({ ... })`\n\nThe error is \"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'map')\". This happens because `order` is undefined. Let me look at the cancel flow...\n\nWait, the test \"cancels a confirmed order\" works fine (it's one of the 12 failures). Looking at the error:\n```\n× OrderService > cancels a confirmed order 5ms\n     → Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'map')\n```\n\nThe error is at `order.items.map` on line 78. But `order` is retrieved from `this.repo.findById(id)` on line 70. If order is undefined, it would have returned undefined at line 71. So order must be defined but order.items is undefined.\n\nLet me look at the flow more carefully. When `createOrder` is called:\n1. `this.repo.create(...)` creates the order with `items`\n2. Event `CREATED` is emitted\n3. Stock is reserved (inventory handler)\n4. `this.billingService.chargePayment(...)` charges payment\n5. `this.repo.update(id, { status: 'confirmed', total: payment.amount })` updates status and total\n\nBut the `update` method in OrderRepository has a bug (Bug 1)! It doesn't preserve existing fields. So after update, the order loses `items`, `customerId`, etc.\n\nSo the flow is:\n1. createOrder creates order with items = [...]\n2. Event CREATED → stock reserved\n3. chargePayment succeeds\n4. repo.update(id, { status: 'confirmed', total: payment.amount }) → this creates a new object with ONLY { status, total, id, updatedAt } — no items!\n5. Now when cancelOrder calls findById, the order has no items → `order.items` is undefined → crash!\n\nSo Bug 1 (OrderRepository.update not preserving fields) is the ROOT CAUSE of the cancelOrder crash.\n\nLet me also check the \"refunds a cancelled order within 30 days\" test:\n```ts\nit('refunds a cancelled order within 30 days', async () => {\n    inventoryService.setStock('SKU-1', 10);\n    await orderService.createOrder('O1', 'C1', [\n      { productId: 'SKU-1', quantity: 1, unitPrice: 100 },\n    ]);\n    await orderService.cancelOrder('O1', 'not needed');\n    const refunded = await orderService.refundOrder('O1');\n    expect(refunded?.status).toBe('refunded');\n});\n```\n\nThis fails because of the same bug — cancelOrder crashes on line 78.\n\nAnd \"refuses to cancel a non-confirmed order\":\n```ts\nit('refuses to cancel a non-confirmed order', async () => {\n    inventoryService.setStock('SKU-1', 10);\n    await orderService.createOrder('O1', 'C1', [\n      { productId: 'SKU-1', quantity: 1, unitPrice: 10 },\n    ]);\n    await orderService.cancelOrder('O1', 'first cancel');\n    const result = await orderService.cancelOrder('O1', 'second cancel');\n    expect(result).toBeUndefined();\n});\n```\n\nFirst cancelOrder should work (order is confirmed at that point), but crashes due to items being lost. The test expects cancelOrder to work the first time and return undefined the second time.\n\nLet me trace:\n1. createOrder → creates order with items → emits CREATED → charges → repo.update (BUG: loses items but status=confirmed) → returns findById (which now has no items)\n2. cancelOrder('O1', 'first cancel') → findById gets order without items → crash on order.items.map\n\nSo yes, Bug 1 cascades into these cancellation test failures.\n\nAlso for \"rejects refund after 30-day window\":\n```ts\nit('rejects refund after 30-day window', async () => {\n    inventoryService.setStock('SKU-1', 10);\n    await orderService.createOrder('O1', 'C1', [\n      { productId: 'SKU-1', quantity: 1, unitPrice: 100 },\n    ]);\n    const thirtyOneDaysMs = 31 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;\n    orderRepo.update('O1', { createdAt: Date.now() - thirtyOneDaysMs });\n    await orderService.cancelOrder('O1', 'very late');\n    await expect(orderService.refundOrder('O1')).rejects.toThrow('Refund window expired');\n});\n```\n\nFirst, createOrder creates order, then update backdates createdAt, then cancelOrder crashes because items were lost. So this test also fails because of Bug 1 + the status field issue.\n\nWait, bug 1, bug 5, and bug 6 are all intertwined. Let me list ALL the bugs systematically:\n\n### Bug 1: OrderRepository.update doesn't preserve existing fields\n**File:** `src/orders/repository.ts:38`\n**Fix:** `const updated = { ...existing, ...updates, id, updatedAt: Date.now() } as Order;`\n\n### Bug 2: BillingService.handleRefund not bound\n**File:** `src/billing/service.ts:25`\n**Fix:** `this.eventBus.on(ORDER_EVENTS.REFUNDED, this.handleRefund.bind(this));`\n\n### Bug 3: calculator.ts rounds intermediate values\n**File:** `src/billing/calculator.ts:20-23`\n**Fix:** Don't round afterDiscount and taxAmount; only round final total.\n\n### Bug 4: Refund window check uses >= instead of >\n**File:** `src/orders/service.ts:99`\n**Fix:** Change `>=` to `>` \n\n### Bug 5: InventoryService cancels stock release because event lacks 'status' field\n**File:** `src/inventory/service.ts:50` — the handler checks `eventWithItems.status !== 'cancelled'` but OrderService doesn't include status in the cancellation event.\n**Fix:** Two options:\n  a. Add `status: 'cancelled'` to the emit in OrderService.cancelOrder\n  b. Remove the status check from InventoryService\n\nSince the tests for inventory emit the event with status, and we shouldn't modify tests, option (a) is the right one.\n\n### Bug 6: (Caused by Bug 1) Items lost during update\nThis is a consequence of Bug 1. Fixing Bug 1 fixes this.\n\nLet me think about whether there are any other issues...\n\nThe \"allows refund on exactly day 30\" test:\n```ts\nit('allows refund on exactly day 30', async () => {\n    inventoryService.setStock('SKU-1', 10);\n    await orderService.createOrder('O1', 'C1', [\n      { productId: 'SKU-1', quantity: 1, unitPrice: 100 },\n    ]);\n    const thirtyDaysMs = 30 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;\n    orderRepo.update('O1', { createdAt: Date.now() - thirtyDaysMs });\n    await orderService.cancelOrder('O1', 'late cancel');\n    const refunded = await orderService.refundOrder('O1');\n    expect(refunded?.status).toBe('refunded');\n});\n```\n\nThis test: createOrder → update createdAt to 30 days ago → cancelOrder → refundOrder → should succeed.\n\nCurrently it fails with `expected undefined to be 'refunded'`. This is because:\n- Fix Bug 1, Bug 4, Bug 5, and Bug 6 for this to work. After fixing:\n  1. createOrder creates order with items → event → payment → update preserves fields\n  2. update backdates createdAt to 30 days ago\n  3. cancelOrder: findById gets order with items, status is confirmed → emit CANCELLED with status → inventory releases stock → update status to cancelled\n  4. refundOrder: findById gets order, status is cancelled, daysSinceCreation = 30\n  5. Bug 4 fix: `>` instead of `>=`, so day 30 passes the check\n  6. issueRefund → updates status to refunded → returns refunded order ✓\n\nAnd \"rejects refund after 30-day window\":\n```ts\nit('rejects refund after 30-day window', async () => {\n    inventoryService.setStock('SKU-1', 10);\n    await orderService.createOrder('O1', 'C1', [\n      { productId: 'SKU-1', quantity: 1, unitPrice: 100 },\n    ]);\n    const thirtyOneDaysMs = 31 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;\n    orderRepo.update('O1', { createdAt: Date.now() - thirtyOneDaysMs });\n    await orderService.cancelOrder('O1', 'very late');\n    await expect(orderService.refundOrder('O1')).rejects.toThrow('Refund window expired');\n});\n```\n\nThis fails with \"Insufficient stock\" in the error, which means `createOrder` is triggering the inventory error. Wait, let me re-read the error:\n```\n× OrderService > rejects refund after 30-day window 1ms\n     → Insufficient stock for product SKU-1\n```\n\nThe error path shows:\n```\n ❯ InventoryService.handleOrderCreated src/inventory/service.ts:27:15\n ❯ EventBus.emit src/events/bus.ts:25:13\n ❯ OrderService.createOrder src/orders/service.ts:45:25\n```\n\nSo during createOrder, the inventory reservation fails. But the test sets `SKU-1` stock to 10 and only requests quantity 1. Why would it fail?\n\nOh wait — the tests share global state through the EventBus! The EventBus has MODULE-LEVEL handlers (globalHandlers). So handlers registered in previous tests still exist. If a previous test registered handlers, the current test would have duplicate or stale handlers.\n\nLooking at the test setup:\n```ts\nbeforeEach(() => {\n    bus = new EventBus();\n    orderRepo = new OrderRepository();\n    inventoryRepo = new InventoryRepository();\n    inventoryService = new InventoryService(inventoryRepo, bus);\n    notifications = new NotificationService();\n    billing = new BillingService(bus, notifications);\n    inventoryService.registerHandlers();\n    billing.registerHandlers();\n    orderService = new OrderService(orderRepo, bus, billing);\n});\n```\n\nEach test creates a new EventBus, but the handlers are stored in the MODULE-LEVEL globalHandlers Map. The `clear()` method exists but is never called in the setup! So handlers accumulate across tests.\n\nBut wait, if the handler registry is global and shared across all EventBus instances, then when test A registers handlers, test B inherits them. The problem is:\n\n1. Test \"creates an order and confirms it\" runs: registers handlers, creates order → works\n2. Test \"reserves stock when order is created\" runs: creates new EventBus but globalHandlers still has previous handlers! New bus.emit will trigger OLD handlers plus NEW handlers.\n\nActually, let me look at the EventBus more carefully:\n```ts\nconst globalHandlers: Map<string, EventHandler[]> = new Map();\n\nexport class EventBus {\n  on(event: string, handler: EventHandler): void {\n    const existing = globalHandlers.get(event) ?? [];\n    existing.push(handler);\n    globalHandlers.set(event, existing);\n  }\n  // ...\n  clear(): 