uses `inventoryService.setStock('SKU-1', 10)` and creates order with qty=1. That should work with just ONE handler.\n\nBut there are 8 handlers (from 8 beforeEaches). Each handler tries to reserve 1 on their own repo. Each repo was set to 10 and had some quantity removed by previous tests' createOrder emissions.\n\nLet me trace through more carefully:\n\n**Test 1 beforeEach**: globalHandlers has 1 inventory handler (handler1)\n**Test 1**: setStock('SKU-1', 10), createOrder (qty=2)\n  - Emit CREATED → handler1 fires → repo1.reserve('SKU-1', 2) → repo1: available=8, reserved=2 ✓\n  - createOrder continues, calls repo.update (Bug 1: loses items), but order is \"confirmed\"\n  - cancelOrder not called in this test\n\n**Test 2 beforeEach**: globalHandlers has 2 inventory handlers (handler1, handler2)\n**Test 2**: setStock('SKU-1', 10) on repo2, createOrder (qty=3)\n  - Emit CREATED → handler1 fires → repo1.reserve('SKU-1', 3) → repo1: available=5, reserved=5 ✓\n  - → handler2 fires → repo2.reserve('SKU-1', 3) → repo2: available=7, reserved=3 ✓\n\n**Test 3 beforeEach**: globalHandlers has 3 handlers\n**Test 3**: setStock('SKU-1', 10) on repo3, createOrder (qty=2)\n  - Emit CREATED → handler1 fires → repo1.reserve('SKU-1', 2) → repo1: available=3, reserved=7 ✓\n  - → handler2 fires → repo2.reserve('SKU-1', 2) → repo2: available=5, reserved=5 ✓\n  - → handler3 fires → repo3.reserve('SKU-1', 2) → repo3: available=8, reserved=2 ✓\n  - createOrder continues, repo.update loses items, cancelOrder crashes\n\n**Test 4 beforeEach**: handlers = 4\n**Test 4**: setStock('SKU-1', 10) on repo4, createOrder (qty=4)\n  - handler1 fires → repo1.reserve('SKU-1', 4) → repo1: available=-1 → FAILS! ❌\n\nThat's the problem! After test 1 (qty=2), repo1 has available=8. After test 2 (qty=3), repo1 has available=5. After test 3 (qty=2), repo1 has available=3. Test 4 tries to reserve 4, but repo1 only has 3 available → Insufficient stock!\n\nSo the duplicate handler issue IS the root cause of some 